IMHO it needs to be looked at on a case by case basis. Three different 
things could be loosely called a case attack:

Passive, just looking at the monster is harmful. The Medusa is in this 
category. The victim must be able to see the monster. A minor would harm 
the monster, reflection would not protect you, but might harm the monster.

"Standard", only "meeting the gaze" of the monster is harmful. Floating 
eyes, umber hulks and probably yeenoghu are in this category. The victim 
and monster must be able to see each other. A minor would harm the 
monster, reflection would not protect you, but might harm the monster.

Active, the monster looking at you is harmful. This his how I imagine 
pyrolisks function. The monster must be able to see the victim. A mirror 
would not harm the monster. Reflection wouldn't protect you, but might 
harm the monster when it uses its attack agaisnt you (if not resistant).

